,title,link,text,language,family
0,Worrorra - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Worrorra,"The Worrorra, also written Worora, are an Aboriginal Australian people of the Kimberley area of north-western Australia. The term is sometimes used to describe speakers of the (Western) Worrorra language, and sometimes groups whose traditional languages are one of the whole group of Worrorran languages.",Worrorra,Australian
1,Donny Woolagoodja - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donny_Woolagoodja,Yornadaiyn (Donny) Woolagoodja (1947-2022) was an Aboriginal Australian artist. He was a member of the Worrorra people of the Kimberley area of Western Australia.,Worrorra,Australian
2,Dirna arrun (We hold it): Recovering collections for Worrorra dance ...,https://indigenousknowledge.unimelb.edu.au/Our-research/seed-funding-projects/2024-projects/dirna-arrun-we-hold-it-recovering-collections-for-worrorra-dance-song-vitality,Investigate and identify content relating to Worrorra dance-songs in identified archived collections. Review photographic collections alongside audio collections to fill gaps in knowledge of Worrorra dance-song repertories. Reconstruct the Worrorra dance-songs with choreography for public performance and reentry to the canon.,Worrorra,Australian
3,Leah Umbagai - Worrorra - YouTube,https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=hkIzGjKJ66c,"Enjoy the videos and music you love, upload original content, and share it all with friends, family, and the world on YouTube.",Worrorra,Australian
4,Worrorra Explained,https://everything.explained.today/Worora_people/,"The Worrorra, also written Worora, are an Aboriginal Australian people of the Kimberley area of north-western Australia. The term is sometimes used to describe speakers of the (Western) Worrorra language, and sometimes groups whose traditional languages are one of the whole group of Worrorran languages.",Worrorra,Australian
5,"Kimberley: Worrorra, Ngarinyin, and Wunambal - Beasts of Legend",https://beastsoflegend.com/bestiary/oceania-2/australian-aboriginal/regional-traditions-and-peoples/kimberley-worrorra-ngarinyin-and-wunambal/,"An overview of Wanjina-Wunggurr Law among Worrorra, Ngarinyin, and Wunambal peoples of the Kimberley, outlining Country, cosmology, rock art, songlines, ceremony, protocols, and practical guidance for respectful engagement.",Worrorra,Australian
6,Worrorra - Cambridge University Press & Assessment,https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/worrorra/3ECA326391BCA5E4A9439C53F4B44DAE/listing,"Worrorra preverbs are semantically verbal, while possessing syntactic properties of both nouns and adverbs. Complex predication, and the lexical category of preverbs it hosts, together constitute one of the defining features of Worrorra in typological terms, and is a characteristic areal feature of north-western Australia generally (see eg ...",Worrorra,Australian
7,Journal for the Anthropological Study of Human Movement | Vol. 21 No. 1 ...,https://jashm.press.uillinois.edu/21.1/treloyn.html,"Introduction There is substantial published material on the musical conventions of Junba dance-songs, as composed and practiced by Ngarinyin, Wunambal, and Worrorra peoples in the Kimberley region of northwest Australia. As a dance-song genre, these musical conventions are inseparable from the dancing that accompanies them. Accordingly, published analyses that address elements of composition ...",Worrorra,Australian
8,Worora — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/worora,"The Worrorra, also known as Worora, are an Aboriginal Australian people traditionally inhabiting the north-west Kimberley coast and adjacent inland areas of Western Australia, including regions around Secure Bay, Walcott Inlet, Collier Bay, and the Prince Regent River. [1][2] Their eponymous language, Worrorra (ISO 639-3: wro), belongs to the non-Pama-Nyungan Worrorran language family and is ...",Worrorra,Australian
9,Sustaining song and spirit | Pursuit by the University of Melbourne,https://pursuit.unimelb.edu.au/articles/sustaining-song-and-spirit,"An Indigenous-led project including a University of Melbourne researcher has revitalised traditional Aboriginal song-and-dance, strengthening the community.",Worrorra,Australian
